  "decision": "map_front",
  "rationale": "The current front is dominated by pipelined_m designs, which are significantly cheaper (1500-2100 LUTs+FFs) than the pipelined designs (2500-5000+). The pipelined_m family achieves the required throughput (89.6 MSPS > 32) and accuracy (14.7-17.8 bits > 12) with much lower area. The front currently spans a wide area range (1500-5171) but the high-area end is inefficient. Mapping the front will allow the code to densely sample the trade-off curve, particularly focusing on the pipelined_m region which offers the best area/accuracy ratio, and to ensure the hypervolume is maximized by covering the full feasible range of the cheaper family.",
  "next_plan": null
}

{
  "decision": "refine",
  "rationale": "The current Pareto front is dominated by `pipelined_m` designs at the low-area end (1500-2183 LUTs+FFs) and `pipelined` at the high-accuracy end. The `pipelined_m` family is significantly more area-efficient (best 1500 vs 2553 for `pipelined`) and meets the throughput constraint (>=32 MSPS) comfortably (89.6 MSPS). The selection rule is `min luts_plus_ffs`, and the current best feasible design is a `pipelined_m` with 1500 LUTs+FFs. To improve hypervolume and potentially find a better minimum-area design, we should focus the search on the `pipelined_m` family in the region where it is most competitive: low `data_width` (19-22), moderate `n_iter` (16-20), and `m=4` (which appears in the top 4 front designs). We should also explore if `m=2` or `m=3` can offer a better trade-off at slightly higher area but potentially better accuracy or Fmax, though `m=4` seems optimal for area. The `pipelined` family is less relevant for the minimum-area selection but contributes to the high-accuracy end of the front; however, since the selection rule prioritizes area, refining `pipelined_m` is the highest-value action. We will narrow the ranges for `pipelined_m` to focus on the promising low-area, high-accuracy region.",
